// longQuestions1.en.js
// HKDSE Biology — Long questions part 1 (Topics 01–13) — English (UK) version
// Loaded when the app language is English (UK). Structure matches longQuestions1.js (zh-HK).
// Each chapter has 15 long questions with model answers (question and answer only, no marking).
// Data structure: { topicNo, name, questions: [ { q, marks, answer } ] }

const LQ1_EN = [
  {
    topicNo: 1,
    name: "Introduction to Biology",
    questions: [
      { q: "A scientist collected some mud samples from the seabed and set up an apparatus to find out whether the mud contains living organisms.\n(a) Name a chemical that can be used to test for carbon dioxide.\n(b) Carbon dioxide was detected from the deep-sea mud. Write down two life characteristics related to this observation.\n(c) Can we conclude that the mud contains living organisms? Why?", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Lime water (calcium hydroxide solution).\n(b) Respiration (produces carbon dioxide); living organisms respire / carry out metabolism.\n(c) Yes. Only living organisms respire continuously, breaking down organic matter to release carbon dioxide; if the mud releases carbon dioxide, it indicates living organisms are respiring inside." },
      { q: "Edward Jenner developed a vaccine against smallpox using the scientific method.\n(a) Outline how he carried out: observation, hypothesis, experiment, conclusion.\n(b) Based on this event, describe the relationship between biology and society.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "(a) Observation: he noticed that people who had had cowpox did not catch smallpox.\nHypothesis: inoculating with the fluid from cowpox pustules could make people immune to smallpox.\nExperiment: he inoculated a healthy boy with cowpox fluid; after recovery, he inoculated the boy with smallpox and the boy did not catch it.\nConclusion: vaccination with cowpox made the boy immune to smallpox; a vaccine against smallpox was developed.\n(b) Biological discoveries can improve human health, reduce disease and death; biological knowledge applied to society benefits humanity; society's needs also drive biological research." },
      { q: "Chi Ho found that the shells of seafood have become thinner in recent years. He thinks that the high concentration of carbon dioxide in sea water makes the sea water acidic, and the acidic water dissolves the shells.\n(a) Write down Chi Ho's observation and hypothesis.\n(b) He collected four types of shells, measured their masses, put them in acids of different strengths for three days and measured the masses again. Calculate the percentage decrease in mass.\n(c) Which type of shell showed the smallest change in mass?\n(d) What conclusion can be drawn?\n(e) Suggest one way to improve this investigation.", marks: "8 marks", answer: "(a) Observation: the shells of seafood have become thinner in recent years.\nHypothesis: the high concentration of carbon dioxide in sea water makes the sea water acidic, and the acidic water dissolves the shells.\n(b) Percentage decrease in mass = (initial mass − mass after 3 days) ÷ initial mass × 100%.\n(c) The mussel (the smallest change in mass).\n(d) Acid dissolves shells, causing a decrease in shell mass; the stronger the acid (the lower the pH), the more it dissolves.\n(e) Increase the number of shells of each type / repeat the experiment / set up a control group (shells not exposed to acid) / control the type of acid, etc." },
      { q: "Redi used two jars (one open, one covered with gauze) containing fresh meat to investigate the origin of maggots.\n(a) Identify the independent variable, dependent variable and control variables.\n(b) What conclusion can be drawn from the results?\n(c) What aspects of the nature of science does this investigation show?", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Independent variable: whether flies could touch the meat.\nDependent variable: whether maggots appeared on the meat.\nControl variables: type of meat, type and size of jars, time of exposure, etc.\n(b) Maggots were produced by flies, not spontaneously from the meat; preventing flies from touching the meat prevented maggots from appearing.\n(c) Science relies on evidence; scientific knowledge is tentative and can be modified; observation is influenced by existing theories, etc." },
      { q: "In medical research on humans, a control group and placebos are needed.\n(a) What is the purpose of setting up a control group?\n(b) Why may it be unethical to test contraceptive pills on humans?\n(c) Suggest a reason why some people oppose testing drugs on animals.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "(a) To provide a basis for comparison, excluding the effect of other factors, ensuring the result is caused only by the drug being tested.\n(b) Giving humans possibly harmful drugs / placebos may endanger their health.\n(c) Animals have feelings and rights; the tests cause suffering to animals / animal physiology differs from humans (give any one reason)." },
      { q: "Pasteur carried out the swan-necked flask experiment in the 1860s.\n(a) What was the purpose of boiling the broth for a period of time?\n(b) What conclusion can be drawn from the result of Apparatus 1 (swan-necked flask)?\n(c) What was the purpose of setting up Apparatus 2 (broken neck)?\n(d) Do Pasteur's results support his hypothesis? Explain.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "(a) To kill the micro-organisms originally present in the broth / sterilise it.\n(b) The broth contains no micro-organisms (as long as no micro-organisms enter, the broth does not go bad); micro-organisms are not spontaneously produced.\n(c) As a control, to show that micro-organisms come from the air (after breaking the neck, micro-organisms can enter the broth).\n(d) Yes. In Apparatus 1, air could enter through the swan neck but micro-organisms could not, so the broth did not go bad; in Apparatus 2, micro-organisms could enter and the broth went bad. This shows micro-organisms come from the air, not spontaneous generation." },
      { q: "Write down the main steps of scientific inquiry, and explain the difference between a hypothesis and a random guess.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Steps: observation → asking a question → making a hypothesis and prediction → designing and carrying out an experiment → analysing results and drawing a conclusion.\nA hypothesis is a testable explanation that can be verified by designing an experiment; a random guess cannot be tested." },
      { q: "A student wanted to find out whether plants watered with glucose solution grow faster than those watered with distilled water. He soaked 100 mung bean seeds to germinate them, selected 20 seedlings of similar height and divided them into two groups, watering one with distilled water and the other with glucose solution.\n(a) Classify the following factors as independent variable, dependent variable or control variables: height of seedlings after one week, type of watering liquid, volume watered daily, amount of light received.\n(b) Identify the experimental group and the control group.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Height of seedlings after one week: dependent variable. Type of watering liquid: independent variable. Volume watered daily: control variable. Amount of light received: control variable.\n(b) The 10 seedlings watered with distilled water: control group. The 10 seedlings watered with glucose solution: experimental group." },
      { q: "Write down the seven typical characteristics of living organisms, and give one example for each.", marks: "7 marks", answer: "1. Nutrition: green plants make their own food by photosynthesis.\n2. Respiration: cells break down food to release energy.\n3. Excretion: removal of metabolic wastes such as carbon dioxide and urea.\n4. Sensitivity (irritability): the leaves of a sensitive plant close when touched.\n5. Movement: animals move about to find food.\n6. Growth: seedlings grow taller and increase in size.\n7. Reproduction: animals produce offspring." },
      { q: "Explain the difference between 'excretion' and 'egestion', giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Excretion is the removal of waste products produced by cell metabolism, e.g. removal of carbon dioxide, urea (in urine).\nEgestion is the removal of undigested food remains from the alimentary canal, e.g. faeces.\nBoth are removal of substances, but their sources differ (metabolic waste vs undigested remains)." },
      { q: "A student wanted to find out whether temperature affects enzyme activity.\n(a) Write down the independent variable, dependent variable and one control variable of this experiment.\n(b) How could the reliability of the results be improved?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) Independent variable: temperature.\nDependent variable: enzyme activity / rate of reaction (e.g. amount of product).\nControl variable: pH, enzyme concentration, substrate concentration, reaction time, etc. (any one).\n(b) Repeat the experiment and use multiple samples, taking the average of the results." },
      { q: "In a controlled experiment, what is the difference between the experimental group and the control group? Why is a control group needed?", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The two groups differ only in the independent variable; all other (control) variables are kept the same.\nThe control group provides a basis for comparison, ensuring that the observed effect is caused by the independent variable rather than other factors, making the experiment a fair test." },
      { q: "Do you think human tissue culture is a living organism? Explain your view.", marks: "2 marks", answer: "It can be argued either way as long as the reasoning is sound.\nFor example: No — tissue culture is not a complete individual and cannot carry out all life processes independently; or Yes — it is made of living cells, and cells are the basic unit of life that can carry out metabolism." },
      { q: "Explain why certain experiments cannot be carried out on humans and model organisms are used instead. Are model organisms exactly the same as humans?", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Using humans may be unethical (harming health) or impractical (long or high-risk experiments), so model organisms (e.g. mice) are used.\nModel organisms have physiology similar to but not identical with humans, so the results may not apply fully to humans and must be inferred with care." },
      { q: "Write down three importance of studying biology (to individuals or society).", marks: "3 marks", answer: "For example:\n1. To understand the structure and functioning of organisms.\n2. Biological knowledge helps develop methods for treatment and conservation, improving quality of life.\n3. To learn to respect organisms and the environment, and to prepare for further study and careers." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 2,
    name: "Molecules of Life",
    questions: [
      { q: "Lactose is present in milk.\n(a) The human body has an enzyme that breaks lactose down into its components. Write down the word equation of this reaction.\n(b) Name the reaction in (a).\n(c) Lactose is a reducing sugar. Outline how you would test for reducing sugar in milk.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Lactose + water ──enzyme──→ glucose + galactose.\n(b) Hydrolysis.\n(c) Take milk and add Benedict's solution, then heat in a boiling water bath for a few minutes; if a reducing sugar is present, a brick-red precipitate appears. (A distilled water control should be set up.)" },
      { q: "Otters are aquatic animals that spend most of their time in water.\n(a) Describe and explain two properties of water that help otters live in water.\n(b) Otters have a thick layer of fat under their fur. Suggest how this fat layer helps otters adapt to an aquatic environment.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "(a) Water has high density, providing buoyancy that helps otters float and move in water; water can dissolve oxygen and nutrients, acting as a medium for metabolism and transport (any two with explanation).\n(b) The fat layer acts as an insulating layer, reducing heat loss and helping otters keep their body temperature in cold water; it also serves as an energy store." },
      { q: "Cholesterol is a lipid.\n(a) Write down two functions of cholesterol in organisms.\n(b) Describe how you would test for lipids in a liquid food sample.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) It is a component of cell membranes; it is used to make lipid hormones (e.g. sex hormones) and vitamin D.\n(b) Place a drop of the liquid sample on a piece of filter paper and leave it to dry; if a permanent translucent spot remains, the sample contains lipids. (The spot disappears when the paper is dipped in an organic solvent.)" },
      { q: "Melamine was once illegally added to infant milk powder.\n(a) Circle the chemical group in melamine that is also found in amino acids.\n(b) Some methods of testing protein content measure the amount of nitrogen. What is the assumption of this method?\n(c) Write down two functions of proteins in the human body.\n(d) Why does melamine-contaminated milk have a greater effect on newborn babies than on adults?", marks: "6 marks", answer: "(a) Amino group (−NH₂).\n(b) The assumption is that all the nitrogen in the sample comes from proteins.\n(c) To build body tissues, growth and repair; to form enzymes, antibodies and some hormones; as an energy source when carbohydrates and lipids are lacking (any two).\n(d) Babies drink more milk per unit body mass, so they take in relatively more melamine; babies' kidneys are immature and less able to excrete waste." },
      { q: "Lipids and proteins perform some similar functions in organisms. Describe these functions and explain how lipids and proteins are suited to these functions.", marks: "9 marks", answer: "Similar functions:\n1. As an energy source — amino acids can enter respiration to produce ATP; fatty acids can also be oxidised to produce ATP.\n2. Growth — phospholipids and proteins are both components of cell membranes, forming new cells.\n3. Regulation — steroid hormones and protein hormones both act as hormones.\n4. Protection — fat protects internal organs; keratin has a protective role.\nSuitability: fat contains many hydrogen atoms and releases more energy on oxidation, so it is suited to energy storage; proteins have diverse shapes and can perform many functions." },
      { q: "Explain the significance of the high specific heat capacity of water to organisms.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Water absorbs a lot of heat before its temperature rises by 1°C; organisms contain much water, so even if the environmental temperature fluctuates, their body temperature remains relatively stable; the temperature of oceans and lakes is quite stable, providing a suitable habitat for aquatic organisms." },
      { q: "Compare DNA and RNA in terms of sugar, bases and structure.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Sugar: DNA contains deoxyribose, RNA contains ribose.\nBases: DNA contains adenine (A), thymine (T), guanine (G) and cytosine (C); RNA has uracil (U) instead of thymine.\nStructure: DNA is a double helix (two polynucleotide chains), RNA is a single chain." },
      { q: "A carbohydrate sample was tested with iodine solution, Benedict's solution, biuret reagent and the grease-spot test. The results were blue-black, a brick-red precipitate, purple and a permanent translucent spot respectively. Deduce the food components in the sample.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Iodine turning blue-black → contains starch.\nBenedict's solution giving a brick-red precipitate → contains reducing sugar.\nBiuret reagent turning purple → contains protein.\nGrease-spot leaving a permanent translucent spot → contains lipids.\nConclusion: the sample contains starch, reducing sugar, protein and lipids at the same time." },
      { q: "Marine mammals are much larger than terrestrial mammals. Explain how the properties of water help marine mammals have such large bodies.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Water has a high density, providing buoyancy that offsets gravity, so a huge body does not need to be fully supported by the skeleton;\nwater is not compressible, providing support;\nwater buffers temperature changes, and the temperature of the sea is stable, helping large animals maintain a constant body temperature.\n(A large body also has a smaller surface-area-to-volume ratio, reducing heat loss.)" },
      { q: "Write down the building blocks of proteins, and explain the reason for the diversity of protein shapes.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The building blocks are amino acids.\nThe 20 kinds of amino acids join in different numbers, types and sequences to form different polypeptide chains; the chains then coil and fold into different shapes, so protein molecules have diverse shapes and can perform many functions." },
      { q: "The table shows the percentages of bases in the DNA of a micro-organism: guanine 18.7%, cytosine 17.1%. Explain why the percentages of guanine and cytosine differ, and state whether the nucleic acid of this micro-organism is DNA.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "In double-stranded DNA, guanine pairs with cytosine, so their percentages should be roughly equal. The large difference in this micro-organism suggests its nucleic acid may be RNA (single-stranded) rather than DNA; RNA contains adenine, uracil, guanine and cytosine, in which guanine and cytosine need not be equal." },
      { q: "Describe and explain the relationship between monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides, giving one example of each.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Monosaccharides are the simplest carbohydrates (e.g. glucose).\nTwo monosaccharides join by condensation (releasing one water molecule) to form a disaccharide (e.g. maltose).\nMany monosaccharides join by condensation to form polysaccharides (e.g. starch, glycogen, cellulose).\nDisaccharides and polysaccharides can be broken down into monosaccharides by hydrolysis; polysaccharides are insoluble in water and are suitable for energy storage or as structural materials." },
      { q: "Write down one function of each of the inorganic ions nitrate, magnesium, calcium, iron and potassium in organisms.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Nitrate: to make proteins, nucleic acids and chlorophyll.\nMagnesium: plants use it to make chlorophyll; a component of bones and teeth.\nCalcium: main component of bones, teeth and shells; needed for muscle contraction and blood clotting.\nIron: to make haemoglobin; plants use it to make chlorophyll.\nPotassium: important for nerve impulse transmission and muscle contraction." },
      { q: "Explain why polysaccharides (such as starch and glycogen) are suitable as energy stores, whereas monosaccharides are not.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Starch and glycogen are insoluble in water, so storing them does not affect the water potential of cells and they do not diffuse out of cells; being large, they are less likely to be lost; they can quickly release glucose by hydrolysis for energy. Monosaccharides are soluble and easily used up, so they are not suitable for long-term storage." },
      { q: "Compare the structures of triglycerides and phospholipids, and explain why phospholipids are the main component of cell membranes.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "A triglyceride consists of one glycerol and three fatty acids; a phospholipid consists of one glycerol, two fatty acids and one phosphate group (one fatty acid is replaced by a phosphate group).\nA phospholipid has a hydrophilic (phosphate) head and a hydrophobic (fatty acid) tail, forming a phospholipid bilayer, which is suited to making up cell membranes." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 3,
    name: "Cellular Organisation",
    questions: [
      { q: "Thyroid epithelial cells make and secrete a protein (thyroglobulin).\n(a) Write down the organelles involved in making and secreting proteins and their functions.\n(b) Explain why palisade mesophyll cells contain fewer mitochondria than thyroid epithelial cells.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Ribosomes: site of protein synthesis; rough endoplasmic reticulum: transport of proteins; Golgi body: modifying, packaging and secreting proteins; nucleus: controlling protein synthesis (any three).\n(b) Palisade mesophyll cells carry out photosynthesis to make food, so their energy demand is lower; thyroid epithelial cells need to synthesize large amounts of protein, so they need many mitochondria to provide ATP." },
      { q: "Comparing a bacterium (prokaryotic cell) with an animal cell (eukaryotic cell).\n(a) Write down two subcellular structures present in human liver cells but not in bacteria.\n(b) Write down two structures present in bacteria but not in animal cells.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) Liver cells have a nucleus (nuclear membrane), mitochondria, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi body and other membrane-bound organelles (any two).\n(b) Bacteria have a cell wall (not made of cellulose), circular DNA free in the cytoplasm, no membrane-bound nucleus, and some have flagella/capsule (any two)." },
      { q: "In a centrifugation experiment, liver tissue is ground up and the cell organelles are separated by a centrifuge, then identified by different tests.\n(a) Why do nuclei sediment first?\n(b) Write down two kinds of molecules that can be detected in the nucleus.\n(c) Explain why a centrifuge can separate different organelles.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) The nucleus has the greatest mass and density, so it sediments first at low speed.\n(b) DNA and proteins (e.g. histones).\n(c) Different organelles have different masses and densities, so they sediment at different speeds and require different speeds to be separated." },
      { q: "Comparing red blood cells and onion epidermal cells:\n(a) Two visible structural differences.\n(b) Write down the outermost structures surrounding red blood cells and onion epidermal cells, and their chemical composition.\n(c) Compare the functions of these outermost structures.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "(a) Red blood cells have no nucleus, no cell wall and are biconcave; onion epidermal cells have a nucleus, cell wall and vacuole.\n(b) Red blood cells: cell membrane (phospholipid bilayer + proteins); onion epidermal cells: cell wall (cellulose) + cell membrane.\n(c) The cell membrane is differentially permeable, controlling substances entering and leaving; the cell wall is freely permeable, protecting the cell and fixing its shape." },
      { q: "Compare light microscopes and electron microscopes in terms of magnification, resolution and suitable specimens.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Light microscope: maximum magnification about 2000×, resolution about 200 nm, can observe living and prepared specimens, simple preparation.\nElectron microscope: maximum magnification about 10,000,000×, resolution about 0.2 nm, can only observe dead specimens (needs dehydration and chemical treatment), complex preparation; transmission type observes internal structure, scanning type observes surface structure." },
      { q: "A student's light microscope is equipped with 5X and 10X eyepieces and 10X and 40X objectives.\n(a) Calculate the highest and lowest total magnifications possible.\n(b) When observing the letter 'b' with the light microscope, what image is seen? Explain.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "(a) Highest: 10X × 40X = 400X; lowest: 5X × 10X = 50X.\n(b) The image seen is 'q'. The light microscope produces an inverted image (upside down and reversed left-right)." },
      { q: "Explain why cells that secrete proteins (such as cells secreting insulin) contain a large amount of rough endoplasmic reticulum.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The rough endoplasmic reticulum has ribosomes on its surface, which is the site of protein synthesis; cells secreting large amounts of protein need many ribosomes and rough endoplasmic reticulum to synthesize and transport proteins, so the rough endoplasmic reticulum is well developed." },
      { q: "Write down the three parts of the cell theory.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "1. All living organisms are made up of one or more cells.\n2. The cell is the basic unit of life (the smallest unit capable of life activities).\n3. Cells come from pre-existing cells." },
      { q: "Describe the functions of the cell membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus, and state the difference between mature red blood cells and typical cells.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Cell membrane: controls substances entering and leaving the cell.\nCytoplasm: supports the organelles and is where many chemical reactions take place.\nNucleus: contains genetic material DNA and controls cell activities.\nMature red blood cells have no nucleus (to make room for haemoglobin); typical body cells have a nucleus." },
      { q: "Write down three structures present in plant cells but not in animal cells, and their functions.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "1. Cell wall (made of cellulose): protects the cell and fixes its shape.\n2. Large central vacuole: contains water and dissolved substances; when full of water it supports the plant.\n3. Chloroplast: contains chlorophyll, absorbing light energy for photosynthesis." },
      { q: "Write down the levels of organisation of organisms (from the simplest to the most complex), illustrating with a human or a plant.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Molecule → organelle → cell → tissue → organ → system → organism.\nExample (human): DNA → nucleus → cardiac muscle cell → cardiac muscle tissue → heart → circulatory system → human.\nExample (plant): chlorophyll → chloroplast → mesophyll cell → mesophyll tissue → leaf → shoot system → plant." },
      { q: "Explain why most animal cells are smaller than plant cells and are irregular in shape.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Animal cells have no cell wall, so their shape is irregular; plant cells are surrounded by a cellulose cell wall, so their shape is fixed and more regular. Animal cells have no large vacuole and are usually smaller." },
      { q: "A student added iodine solution when observing onion epidermal cells with a light microscope. Explain the purpose of adding iodine solution.", marks: "2 marks", answer: "Iodine solution stains structures such as the nucleus so that they become clearer and easier to observe (iodine also turns blue-black in the presence of starch)." },
      { q: "Describe the location and function of ribosomes in a cell, and explain why all living cells contain many ribosomes.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Ribosomes are very small organelles without a membrane, free in the cytoplasm or attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum.\nRibosomes are the site of protein synthesis; all living cells need proteins to build structures and perform functions (such as enzymes), so they all contain many ribosomes." },
      { q: "Write down the function of lysosomes, and give an example of their action.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Lysosomes are sac-like structures surrounded by a single membrane, containing various hydrolytic enzymes that can break down proteins, lipids, carbohydrates and nucleic acids; they fuse with worn-out organelles or foreign particles and break them down, recycling useful materials (e.g. clearing ageing organelles)." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 4,
    name: "Movement of Substances across Cell Membrane",
    questions: [
      { q: "Fresh lettuce becomes soft after being soaked in concentrated salt water. Explain this phenomenon.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Concentrated salt water is a hypertonic solution whose water potential is lower than that of the cell contents of the lettuce.\nWater moves by osmosis from the cell contents to the concentrated salt water.\nThe cells lose water, the vacuole shrinks, and the cytoplasm separates from the cell wall (plasmolysis).\nThe cells lose turgor pressure, so the lettuce becomes soft." },
      { q: "Potato cylinders are immersed in sucrose solutions of different concentrations and their masses are measured after two hours.\n(a) Explain why the mass increases in some concentrations and decreases in others.\n(b) How can the water potential of the potato cells be found?", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) In a low-concentration (hypotonic) solution, water enters the cells by osmosis and the mass increases; in a high-concentration (hypertonic) solution, water leaves the cells by osmosis and the mass decreases.\n(b) Find the concentration at which the mass does not change; at this concentration the water potential of the sucrose solution equals that of the potato cells, because there is no net movement of water." },
      { q: "Based on the fluid mosaic model of the cell membrane, explain the following:\n(a) Fatty acids (non-polar molecules) can diffuse across the cell membrane into epithelial cells.\n(b) Amino acids (polar molecules) can enter epithelial cells from the intestinal lumen, but cannot diffuse back to the lumen.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) The cell membrane consists of a phospholipid bilayer, with hydrophobic fatty acid tails in the middle; non-polar molecules (fatty acids) can dissolve in the phospholipid bilayer and diffuse directly across.\n(b) Amino acids are polar molecules that cannot pass directly through the phospholipid bilayer; they enter the cell by carrier proteins / active transport. As active transport is one-way (against the concentration gradient), amino acids that have entered cannot diffuse back to the lumen (diffusion requires moving down the gradient)." },
      { q: "Write down three differences between active transport and diffusion.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "1. Active transport moves against the concentration gradient (from low to high); diffusion moves down the concentration gradient.\n2. Active transport requires energy (ATP); diffusion does not.\n3. Active transport requires carrier proteins; simple diffusion does not." },
      { q: "Explain why animal cells burst in a hypotonic solution but plant cells do not.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The water potential of a hypotonic solution is higher than that inside the cell, so water enters the cell by osmosis.\nAnimal cells have no cell wall; they swell and eventually burst.\nPlant cells have a rigid cell wall that stops the cell from expanding without limit; the cell becomes turgid (turgor pressure rises) without bursting, providing support for the plant." },
      { q: "Write down four factors affecting the rate of diffusion and explain how each affects it.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "1. Concentration gradient: the steeper the gradient, the faster the diffusion.\n2. Temperature: the higher the temperature, the greater the kinetic energy of molecules, the faster the diffusion.\n3. Distance for diffusion: the shorter the distance, the faster the diffusion.\n4. Size of molecules: the smaller the molecules, the faster the diffusion. (Or surface area: the larger the area, the faster.)" },
      { q: "Explain the difference between osmosis and diffusion.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Osmosis is the movement of water molecules through a selectively permeable membrane from a high water potential to a low water potential; diffusion is the movement of any particles from a high concentration to a low concentration. Osmosis involves only water molecules and requires a selectively permeable membrane; diffusion does not require a selectively permeable membrane (and can involve any particles)." },
      { q: "Root hair cells of plants can absorb mineral ions from the soil even when the ion concentration in the soil is low. Explain how this is achieved.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Root hair cells use active transport, consuming energy (ATP) and using carrier proteins, to move mineral ions against the concentration gradient from the low-concentration soil into the cell; therefore even when the ion concentration in the soil is low, absorption can still occur." },
      { q: "Explain the significance of the 'differential permeability' (selective permeability) of the cell membrane to the cell.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The cell membrane allows certain substances (such as oxygen, carbon dioxide and water) to pass through while restricting others (such as large molecules and ions), enabling the cell to control its internal composition and maintain a stable internal environment (homeostasis), which is important for survival." },
      { q: "A student placed onion epidermal cells in a hypertonic sucrose solution and observed plasmolysis.\n(a) Explain plasmolysis.\n(b) What happens if the cells are put back into distilled water?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) The water potential of the hypertonic solution is low, so the cells lose water, the vacuole shrinks, and the cytoplasm together with the cell membrane separates from the cell wall, giving plasmolysis.\n(b) In distilled water the water potential is high, so water enters the cells by osmosis, the vacuole expands, and the cytoplasm returns to press against the cell wall (deplasmolysis)." },
      { q: "Write down the main components of the cell membrane, and explain how phospholipid molecules are arranged in the membrane.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The cell membrane is mainly composed of a phospholipid bilayer and proteins.\nPhospholipids are arranged in a bilayer: the hydrophilic phosphate heads face the two outer (aqueous) sides, and the hydrophobic fatty acid tails face the middle, forming a selective barrier; proteins are embedded in or span the phospholipid bilayer." },
      { q: "Explain why oxygen can pass directly through the phospholipid bilayer of the cell membrane, whereas glucose needs carrier proteins.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Oxygen is a small non-polar molecule that can dissolve in the hydrophobic core of the phospholipid bilayer and diffuse directly across.\nGlucose is a larger polar molecule that is repelled by the hydrophobic core of the bilayer, so it cannot pass directly; it needs carrier proteins in the membrane (facilitated diffusion or active transport) to enter the cell." },
      { q: "Red blood cells of an organism were placed in three solutions P, Q and R; their masses changed as follows: P increased, Q unchanged, R decreased.\n(a) Classify each solution as hypotonic, isotonic or hypertonic relative to the cell contents.\n(b) Explain why the mass of the red blood cells increased in solution P.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) P is hypotonic (water enters the cells); Q is isotonic (water potentials equal); R is hypertonic (water leaves the cells).\n(b) The water potential of P is higher than that of the cell contents, so water enters the red blood cells by osmosis; the cells take in water and swell, so their mass increases." },
      { q: "Name one mechanism by which the kidneys reabsorb glucose, and state one difference between this mechanism and diffusion.", marks: "2 marks", answer: "Mechanism: active transport.\nDifference: active transport moves against the concentration gradient and requires energy; diffusion moves down the concentration gradient and requires no energy (or active transport requires carrier proteins)." },
      { q: "Explain why fresh potato cylinders become hard in distilled water but soft in concentrated salt water.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Distilled water has a high water potential, so water enters the cells by osmosis, the cells swell, turgor pressure rises, and the cylinders become hard.\nConcentrated salt water has a low water potential, so water leaves the cells by osmosis, the cells lose water, turgor pressure falls (plasmolysis), and the cylinders become soft." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 5,
    name: "Metabolism and Enzymes",
    questions: [
      { q: "A student investigated the effect of pH on catalase extracted from mung bean sprouts. Explain why the paper discs rise to the surface of the solution, and state the optimum pH range of catalase.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide, releasing oxygen.\nWhen enough oxygen is produced, the bubbles lift the paper discs to the surface of the solution.\nThe optimum pH is about 7–9.\nTo estimate more accurately, buffer solutions with pH between 7 and 9 and smaller intervals should be used and the experiment repeated. (Note: keeping at 4°C does not denature the enzyme.)" },
      { q: "In the 'ginger milk pudding' experiment, fresh ginger juice curdles milk, but boiled ginger juice does not. Explain this phenomenon and the role of temperature.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Ginger juice contains an enzyme that curdles milk.\nEnzymes are proteins, and high temperature (boiling) denatures the protein.\nTherefore the enzyme in boiled ginger juice loses its catalytic activity and the milk does not curdle.\nMilk only curdles at about 65°C because this temperature suits the enzyme's activity; too high a temperature denatures the enzyme, while too low a temperature gives low enzyme activity." },
      { q: "Write down four characteristics of enzymes.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "1. Enzymes are biological catalysts and their chemical nature is protein.\n2. Enzymes are specific (catalyse only particular substrates).\n3. Enzymes lower the activation energy of reactions.\n4. Enzymes can be reused and are not used up in reactions; enzyme activity is affected by temperature and pH." },
      { q: "Explain how the lock-and-key hypothesis explains the specificity of enzymes.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The active site of an enzyme has a specific shape, and only a substrate with a perfectly complementary shape can bind to it, just as only the right 'key' can open a 'lock'.\nThe substrate binds to the enzyme to form an enzyme–substrate complex, which then breaks down and releases the products; the enzyme is unchanged after the reaction and can bind to more substrate.\nTherefore an enzyme can only catalyse the particular substrate whose shape is complementary to its active site." },
      { q: "In an enzyme-catalysed reaction, the initial rate increases as the temperature rises, but drops sharply beyond the optimum temperature. Explain.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "As the temperature rises, the kinetic energy of the enzyme and substrate increases and collisions become more frequent, so the reaction rate increases.\nThe rate is fastest at the optimum temperature.\nBeyond the optimum temperature, high temperature denatures the enzyme protein, changing the shape of the active site so it cannot bind to the substrate; the rate drops sharply and irreversibly." },
      { q: "Write down four applications of enzymes in daily life and industry.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "1. Meat tenderiser / contact lens cleaner (protease).\n2. Biological washing powder (protease).\n3. Urine-glucose test strips (glucose oxidase).\n4. Making fruit juice (pectinase), lactose-free milk (lactase), biofuels." },
      { q: "Explain why, in an enzyme-catalysed reaction, the rate no longer rises when the substrate concentration increases beyond a certain level.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "As the substrate concentration increases, more substrate binds to the active sites of the enzymes and the rate rises; when all the active sites are occupied by substrate (saturated), increasing the substrate further cannot increase the number of enzyme–substrate complexes, so the rate stays constant." },
      { q: "Compare catabolism and anabolism, giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Catabolism breaks complex molecules into simpler substances, usually releasing energy, e.g. glucose being broken down into carbon dioxide and water.\nAnabolism builds simple substances into complex molecules, usually absorbing energy, e.g. amino acids being synthesised into proteins." },
      { q: "Pepsin works in the stomach.\n(a) Write down the optimum pH environment of pepsin.\n(b) Explain why pepsin activity decreases when it enters the weakly alkaline environment of the small intestine.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "(a) Strongly acidic (about pH 2).\n(b) Enzymes are proteins; an extreme pH (alkaline) changes the shape of the enzyme, causing denaturation, so the active site cannot bind to the substrate and activity decreases." },
      { q: "A student heated a catalase solution to 100°C, cooled it to room temperature, and then mixed it with hydrogen peroxide. No bubbles were produced. Explain.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Heating to 100°C denatures the enzyme protein, permanently changing the shape of the active site so it cannot bind to the substrate; cooling does not restore the activity, so no bubbles (oxygen) are produced." },
      { q: "Explain how enzymes lower the activation energy of reactions, and why this is important to cellular metabolism.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Enzymes bind to the substrate and provide an alternative reaction pathway, lowering the activation energy required, so the reaction can proceed quickly even at low temperature.\nThis is important because the temperature inside cells is low; without enzymes, metabolic reactions would be too slow to sustain life." },
      { q: "Write down the characteristics of catalysts, and explain the similarities and differences between enzymes and general catalysts.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Characteristics of catalysts: speed up reactions, are not used up, and do not change the types of products.\nSimilarity: enzymes are catalysts that speed up reactions and are not used up.\nDifference: enzymes are proteins affected by temperature and pH, are specific, and act inside organisms; general catalysts (such as metals) are not limited by biological conditions and have no specificity." },
      { q: "Write down four factors affecting enzyme activity, and explain how pH affects enzyme activity.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Factors: temperature, pH, enzyme concentration, substrate concentration, inhibitors, etc.\nEffect of pH: each enzyme has an optimum pH at which activity is highest; pH deviating from the optimum changes the charge and shape of the enzyme molecule, and extreme pH causes denaturation, decreasing activity." },
      { q: "A student wanted to investigate the effect of enzyme concentration on the rate of reaction.\n(a) Write down the independent variable, dependent variable and control variables.\n(b) Predict and explain the results.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Independent variable: enzyme concentration.\nDependent variable: rate of reaction (e.g. amount of product or number of bubbles).\nControl variables: temperature, pH, substrate concentration, time, etc.\n(b) As the enzyme concentration increases, the rate of reaction rises; assuming substrate is in excess, the rate is roughly proportional to the enzyme concentration; if substrate becomes limiting, the rate stops rising." },
      { q: "Explain why low temperature lowers enzyme activity but usually does not permanently inactivate the enzyme, whereas high temperature does.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "At low temperature the kinetic energy of molecules is low and collisions are few, so enzyme activity is low but the enzyme is not destroyed; when a suitable temperature is restored, activity recovers.\nHigh temperature breaks down the protein structure (denaturation), permanently changing the shape of the active site, so the activity cannot be restored." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 6,
    name: "Food and Humans",
    questions: [
      { q: "Compare the energy values of carbohydrates, lipids and proteins, and explain why the body uses fat as its main long-term energy store.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Carbohydrates and proteins provide about 17.1 kJ per gram; lipids provide about 38.9 kJ per gram.\nLipids provide the most energy per gram and occupy relatively little space, so they are suitable for long-term storage.\nExcess energy is mainly stored as fat in adipose tissue.\nGlycogen storage is limited (in the liver and muscles), so fat is the main long-term energy store." },
      { q: "Which diseases are caused by a deficiency of vitamin A, vitamin C, vitamin D, iron and iodine respectively?", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Vitamin A → night blindness.\nVitamin C → scurvy.\nVitamin D → rickets (soft bones in children).\nIron → anaemia (insufficient haemoglobin).\nIodine → goitre (enlargement of the thyroid gland)." },
      { q: "Write down the seven food components and one function of each.", marks: "7 marks", answer: "1. Carbohydrates: main energy source.\n2. Lipids: energy store, insulation, protection of internal organs.\n3. Proteins: growth and repair of body tissues.\n4. Vitamins: maintaining normal physiological functions (deficiency causes deficiency diseases).\n5. Minerals: forming bones, haemoglobin, etc.\n6. Water: solvent, temperature regulation, transport medium.\n7. Dietary fibre: increases food bulk, stimulates gut movement, helps defecation." },
      { q: "Explain why we need to take in dietary fibre even though the human body cannot digest it.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Dietary fibre (cellulose) cannot be digested, but it increases food bulk, stimulates gut movement and helps defecation, preventing constipation, which is important for gut health." },
      { q: "An athlete drinks a sports drink after a competition. Explain the benefits of the water and carbohydrate (e.g. glucose) in the sports drink.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Water: replaces the water lost in sweat, preventing dehydration, and acts as a medium for transport and metabolism.\nGlucose: quickly replenishes energy for respiration in muscles, helping recovery and maintaining the blood glucose level." },
      { q: "Write down one function of iron, calcium, iodine and magnesium in the human body, and the problem caused by a deficiency of each.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Iron: forms haemoglobin; deficiency → anaemia.\nCalcium: forms bones and teeth; deficiency → rickets / osteoporosis.\nIodine: forms thyroxine; deficiency → goitre.\nMagnesium: component of bones and teeth / plant chlorophyll; deficiency in humans affects muscle and nerve function." },
      { q: "Write down the methods and positive results for testing glucose, reducing sugar, starch, lipids and proteins.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Glucose: urine-glucose test strip (colour change of the test end).\nReducing sugar: Benedict's test (brick-red precipitate).\nStarch: iodine test (brown → blue-black).\nLipids: grease-spot test (permanent translucent spot).\nProtein: urine-protein test strip / biuret reagent (colour change / purple)." },
      { q: "Explain why a balanced diet is important to health, and write down two factors affecting an individual's dietary requirements.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "A balanced diet means taking in various kinds of food in proper proportions, avoiding excess or deficiency; deficiency causes deficiency diseases, while excess leads to obesity and cardiovascular diseases.\nDietary requirements vary from person to person, affected by age, sex, daily activity level and physical condition." },
      { q: "Compare autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition, giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Autotrophic nutrition: organisms can carry out photosynthesis, synthesising organic food from simple inorganic substances, e.g. green plants.\nHeterotrophic nutrition: organisms need to take in ready-made organic food to obtain nutrients, e.g. animals and fungi." },
      { q: "Why are vitamins needed only in small amounts, yet a deficiency still causes disease? Give two examples.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Vitamins do not provide energy but act as accessory substances in metabolism (such as coenzymes), so only small amounts are needed; the body cannot synthesize all vitamins and must obtain them from food.\nDeficiency of vitamin C → scurvy; deficiency of vitamin D → rickets. (Other examples are also acceptable.)" },
      { q: "A student wanted to compare the vitamin C contents of two fruit juices. Write down the experimental design (including the principle).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Principle: vitamin C decolourises DCPIP.\nMethod: add the juice drop by drop to a fixed volume of blue DCPIP solution, recording the number of drops needed to decolourise it; the fewer drops needed, the higher the vitamin C concentration.\nUse a standard vitamin C solution as a control, and repeat the experiment." },
      { q: "Explain why eating a high-fat diet increases the risk of cardiovascular diseases.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "A high-fat diet raises blood cholesterol; fatty substances are deposited on the inner walls of arteries to form atheromas (atherosclerosis), narrowing the lumen and increasing the risk of coronary heart disease and stroke." },
      { q: "Write down the classification of vitamins (water-soluble / fat-soluble), giving two examples of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Water-soluble vitamins: vitamin B, vitamin C.\nFat-soluble vitamins: vitamin A, vitamin D, vitamin E, vitamin K.\nFat-soluble vitamins can be stored in adipose tissue and may accumulate if taken in excess; excess water-soluble vitamins are excreted in urine." },
      { q: "Explain why water has an energy value of zero but is still very important to life.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Water provides no energy, but it is a good solvent and transport medium, a reactant in metabolic reactions (e.g. photosynthesis), a coolant that regulates body temperature, buffers temperature changes and provides support; therefore it is indispensable to life." },
      { q: "A student tested a sucrose solution with Benedict's solution and no brick-red precipitate appeared. Explain, and suggest how to confirm the presence of sucrose.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Sucrose is not a reducing sugar, so it gives a negative result with Benedict's test.\nFirst hydrolyse the sucrose (add dilute hydrochloric acid and heat, then neutralise with sodium hydroxide, or add sucrase), then test with Benedict's solution; if a brick-red precipitate appears, it shows that sucrose has been hydrolysed into reducing sugars (glucose and fructose)." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 7,
    name: "Human Nutrition",
    questions: [
      { q: "A patient has had his gall bladder (structure A) removed. Explain why he cannot digest fatty food as usual after the operation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Bile produced by the liver cannot be stored in the gall bladder.\nWhen food enters the duodenum, insufficient bile is released to emulsify the fat in the food.\nThe fat cannot be emulsified, so the surface area available for lipase action is reduced.\nTherefore the fat cannot be digested effectively." },
      { q: "What features of the villi of the small intestine help the absorption of digested food?", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Villi are finger-like projections of the small intestine wall, providing a large surface area for absorbing food.\nThe epithelium of the villi is very thin (a single layer of cells), shortening the diffusion/transport distance for digested food.\nThe villi have a rich network of capillaries, which quickly carry away absorbed food, maintaining a steep concentration gradient for absorption.\nThe villi contain lacteals that absorb the products of fat digestion." },
      { q: "Write down the digestion of starch, proteins and lipids in the alimentary canal, including the enzymes involved and the final products.", marks: "7 marks", answer: "Starch: salivary amylase in the mouth (starch → maltose); pancreatic amylase (starch → maltose); carbohydrate-digesting enzymes on the epithelial cell membrane of the small intestine (disaccharides → monosaccharides). Final product: glucose.\nProteins: pepsin in the stomach (proteins → peptides); trypsin (proteins → peptides → amino acids); proteases on the small intestine wall (peptides → amino acids). Final product: amino acids.\nLipids: bile salts emulsify fat; pancreatic lipase (lipids → fatty acids + glycerol). Final products: fatty acids and glycerol." },
      { q: "Write down four functions of the liver.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "1. Regulation of blood glucose (when blood glucose is high, glucose → glycogen; when low, glycogen → glucose).\n2. Deamination (excess amino acids → urea).\n3. Secretion of bile (stored in the gall bladder).\n4. Storage of glycogen / iron / fat-soluble vitamins; breaking down toxic substances (e.g. alcohol, drugs)." },
      { q: "Explain how peristalsis helps food move along the alimentary canal, and write down the main function of the large intestine.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Peristalsis is the wave-like contraction of the muscles of the gut wall, pushing food forward and mixing food with digestive enzymes.\nThe large intestine mainly absorbs water and forms faeces." },
      { q: "Write down the main types of teeth and their functions.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Incisors: cutting food.\nCanines: tearing food.\nPremolars / molars: grinding food.\n(Adults have 32 teeth in total, with the dental formula 2123 on each side.)" },
      { q: "Explain the function of bile, and why bile can help digest fat even though it contains no enzymes.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Bile contains no digestive enzymes, but it emulsifies fat (breaks it into small droplets), increasing the surface area of the fat so that pancreatic lipase can break it down more effectively.\nEmulsification is a physical process that does not require enzymes." },
      { q: "A diabetic patient's small intestine cells cannot absorb glucose effectively. Explain how glucose is usually absorbed by the villus cells, and the difference between this mechanism and diffusion.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Glucose is taken up into the villus cells by active transport (against the concentration gradient) using carrier proteins, and can also enter by facilitated diffusion down the gradient.\nDifference between active transport and diffusion: active transport moves against the concentration gradient and requires energy (ATP) and carrier proteins; diffusion moves down the gradient and requires no energy (simple diffusion needs no carrier proteins)." },
      { q: "Write down the difference between 'physical digestion' and 'chemical digestion', giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Physical digestion: breaks food into smaller pieces and increases surface area without changing the chemical composition, e.g. chewing by teeth, churning by the stomach wall, emulsification of fat by bile salts.\nChemical digestion: enzymes break large molecules into smaller molecules, changing the chemical composition, e.g. amylase breaking down starch." },
      { q: "Explain why the small intestine is the main site for absorbing nutrients.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The small intestine is long, so food stays there for a long time; the intestinal wall has many villi and microvilli, giving a large surface area; the villus wall is thin (a single layer of cells), shortening the diffusion distance; the villi have a rich supply of capillaries and lacteals, quickly carrying away absorbed substances and maintaining a concentration gradient." },
      { q: "A student added equal amounts of saliva to starch solutions and placed them at different temperatures for a period of time, then tested with iodine solution.\n(a) Predict and explain the results.\n(b) Write down the variables that should be controlled.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) At the optimum temperature (about 37°C) salivary amylase is most active and the starch is broken down fastest, so the iodine does not turn blue-black; at low temperature enzyme activity is low, so the iodine still turns blue-black; at high temperature the enzyme is denatured, so the starch is not broken down and the iodine turns blue-black.\n(b) Control variables: starch concentration, amount of saliva, pH, reaction time, etc." },
      { q: "Write down the final products of protein digestion, and explain why these products are important to the body.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The final products are amino acids.\nThe amino acids are absorbed by the villi and transported by the blood to all parts of the body, where they are used to synthesise new proteins (growth and repair of tissues, forming enzymes and antibodies, etc.)." },
      { q: "Explain why eating a high-fibre diet helps prevent constipation.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Dietary fibre cannot be digested, and it increases the bulk of faeces, absorbs water and stimulates gut movement, making food remains and faeces easier to pass out of the body, thus helping to prevent constipation." },
      { q: "A student wanted to find out the optimum pH of digestive enzyme X.\n(a) Design the experimental procedure.\n(b) Write down the independent variable, dependent variable and control variables.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Add equal amounts of enzyme X to buffer solutions of different pH, add equal amounts of substrate, and after the same temperature and time measure the amount of product (or the rate of reaction).\n(b) Independent variable: pH.\nDependent variable: amount of product / rate of reaction.\nControl variables: temperature, enzyme concentration, substrate concentration, reaction time." },
      { q: "Explain the role of salivary amylase in the mouth, and why its activity decreases after it reaches the stomach.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Salivary amylase breaks down starch into maltose in the mouth.\nAfter reaching the stomach, the strongly acidic environment denatures salivary amylase (whose optimum pH is about neutral) or greatly reduces its activity, so starch is no longer broken down." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 8,
    name: "Gas Exchange in Humans",
    questions: [
      { q: "Describe and explain two features of the alveoli that help gas exchange.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The alveolar wall is very thin (a single layer of cells), shortening the diffusion distance for gases and speeding up gas exchange.\nThe alveoli have a large surface area (numerous small sacs), providing a large surface for gas exchange.\nThe alveolar surface is moist, dissolving gases and helping diffusion.\nThe alveoli are surrounded by a rich network of capillaries, quickly carrying away oxygen and bringing carbon dioxide, maintaining a steep concentration gradient." },
      { q: "A patient's lung has a layer of hardened dust deposited in the air sacs. Suggest two ways in which this disease may hinder gas exchange.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The dust forms a barrier, increasing the diffusion distance and slowing the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide.\nThe inner surface of the air sacs is covered by dust, reducing the surface area available for diffusion.\nThe hardened dust reduces the elasticity of the lungs, so when the lungs expand, the lung capacity is smaller and ventilation is reduced." },
      { q: "Describe the mechanism of inhalation and exhalation, including the changes in the diaphragm, ribs and the volume of the thorax.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Inhalation: the diaphragm contracts and moves down, the ribs move upwards and outwards, the volume of the thorax increases, the air pressure in the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure, and air enters.\nExhalation: the diaphragm relaxes and moves up, the ribs move downwards and inwards, the volume of the thorax decreases, the air pressure in the lungs rises above atmospheric pressure, and air leaves." },
      { q: "Explain how oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged between the alveoli and the blood, and how carbon dioxide is transported in the blood.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "The oxygen concentration in the alveoli is higher than in the blood, so oxygen dissolves in the thin water film and diffuses across the alveolar wall and capillary wall into the blood, combining with haemoglobin.\nThe carbon dioxide concentration in the capillaries is higher than in the alveoli, so carbon dioxide diffuses into the alveolar air.\nTransport of carbon dioxide: dissolved in plasma (5%); combined with haemoglobin (10%); reacting with water to form hydrogencarbonate ions (85%)." },
      { q: "Write down the function of the cilia and mucus on the inner wall of the trachea and bronchi.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Mucus traps inhaled dust and micro-organisms; the beating of cilia moves the mucus carrying dust upwards to the throat, where it is coughed out or swallowed, preventing pathogens and dust from reaching the lungs." },
      { q: "Compare 'breathing' (ventilation) and 'respiration'.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Breathing (ventilation) is the movement of gases into and out of the body, which takes place in the lungs and involves inhalation and exhalation.\nRespiration is the process of breaking down food in cells (mitochondria) to release energy.\nThe two have similar names but completely different meanings." },
      { q: "Explain why the rate and depth of breathing increase during vigorous exercise.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "During exercise the muscles need more energy and carry out more respiration, producing more carbon dioxide.\nThe rise in blood carbon dioxide and the fall in pH stimulate the respiratory centre in the medulla oblongata.\nThe medulla sends more impulses to the respiratory muscles (intercostal muscles and diaphragm), making breathing faster and deeper, taking in more oxygen and expelling more carbon dioxide." },
      { q: "Write down the differences in composition between inhaled air and exhaled air, and explain the reasons.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Compared with inhaled air, exhaled air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide, and more water vapour.\nReasons: body cells carry out respiration, consuming oxygen and producing carbon dioxide; the exhaled air is also moistened as it passes through the respiratory tract." },
      { q: "Describe how haemoglobin in red blood cells carries oxygen, and how oxyhaemoglobin releases oxygen in the tissues.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In the lungs the oxygen partial pressure is high, so oxygen combines with haemoglobin to form oxyhaemoglobin.\nIn the tissues the oxygen partial pressure is low, so oxyhaemoglobin dissociates and releases oxygen into the body cells.\nHaemoglobin enables the blood to carry far more oxygen than could be dissolved." },
      { q: "Explain why athletes generally have a larger vital capacity, and how this helps their performance.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Regular exercise strengthens the respiratory muscles and increases vital capacity.\nA larger vital capacity allows more air to be taken in per breath, supplying more oxygen to the muscles for respiration, delaying fatigue and improving performance." },
      { q: "A student tested exhaled air with lime water and found it turned milky. Explain what this result shows.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Exhaled air contains more carbon dioxide; carbon dioxide reacts with lime water (calcium hydroxide solution) to form insoluble calcium carbonate, making the lime water turn milky. This shows that respiration produces carbon dioxide." },
      { q: "Explain why smoking increases the risk of lung diseases.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Tar in the smoke is deposited in the lungs, damaging the alveolar walls and cilia, reducing the surface area for gas exchange and increasing the diffusion distance;\nCarbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin, reducing the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood;\nCarcinogens increase the risk of lung cancer; smoke stimulates excess mucus secretion and narrows the airways, causing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, etc." },
      { q: "Describe three structural features of the alveoli and explain how each is related to its function of gas exchange.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "1. Thin walls (single layer of cells): shorten the diffusion distance and speed up gas exchange.\n2. Large surface area (numerous small alveolar sacs): provide a large surface for gas exchange.\n3. Moist surface: dissolves oxygen and carbon dioxide, facilitating diffusion.\n4. Surrounded by a rich network of capillaries: quickly carries away oxygen and brings carbon dioxide, maintaining a concentration gradient (any three)." },
      { q: "Explain why the condition of patients with asthma may worsen in areas of severe air pollution.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Air pollutants (such as suspended particulates and sulphur dioxide) irritate the airways, causing bronchial muscles to contract and narrowing the airways, increasing airflow resistance;\npollutants stimulate excess mucus secretion and inflammation, further narrowing the airways and making breathing difficult, worsening the asthma." },
      { q: "Write down the conditions required for a gas exchange surface, illustrating with the alveoli.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Conditions: large surface area, thin walls, moist surface, close contact with the blood (rich capillaries), maintaining a concentration gradient.\nThe alveolar walls are thin and moist, the surface area is large, and there is a rich network of capillaries, so they are suitable as a gas exchange surface." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 9,
    name: "Transport of Substances in Humans",
    questions: [
      { q: "Compare the blood pressure in arteries and veins, and give two reasons to explain the difference.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The blood pressure in veins is much lower than that in arteries.\nReason one: blood in arteries is directly pumped by the heart, while blood in veins is not.\nReason two: after flowing over a long distance, the blood has experienced a lot of friction/resistance.\n(Or: some fluid is lost when tissue fluid is formed.)" },
      { q: "Describe how tissue fluid is formed, and explain how the lymphatic system returns excess tissue fluid to the blood.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "At the arterial end of a capillary, the blood pressure is higher than the pressure of the fluid around the body cells, so water, minerals, glucose and other substances in the plasma are forced out of the capillary wall into the intercellular spaces, forming tissue fluid.\nAt the venous end of the capillary, the blood pressure is lower than that of the tissue fluid; as plasma proteins and blood cells remain in the blood, the tissue fluid has a higher water potential than the blood, so most water returns to the blood by osmosis.\nThe remaining excess tissue fluid enters the lymphatic capillaries and becomes lymph.\nLymph flows back through lymphatic vessels and finally returns to the heart via veins near the neck." },
      { q: "Describe the structural features of red blood cells and their adaptations related to oxygen transport.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Red blood cells are biconcave disc-shaped (increasing the surface area for gas diffusion).\nThey are flat (shortening the distance for oxygen to reach the inner part of the cell).\nThey contain haemoglobin (carrying oxygen).\nThey have no nucleus and few organelles when mature (providing more space for haemoglobin)." },
      { q: "Describe the pathway of blood through the heart and blood vessels (pulmonary circulation and systemic circulation).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Pulmonary circulation: right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs (gas exchange) → pulmonary vein → left atrium.\nSystemic circulation: left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body → superior/inferior vena cavae → right atrium.\nThe wall of the left ventricle is thicker than that of the right ventricle because the left ventricle has to pump blood to the whole body (a longer distance)." },
      { q: "Write down the structural features of arteries, veins and capillaries and their functions.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Arteries: thick walls withstand high blood pressure; muscle contraction regulates blood flow; elastic fibres maintain blood pressure.\nVeins: larger lumen reduces resistance to blood flow; valves prevent backflow, ensuring one-way flow back to the heart.\nCapillaries: branch into a huge network increasing the surface area for exchange; walls are only one cell thick, shortening the diffusion distance; slow blood flow allows more time for exchange." },
      { q: "Explain why capillaries are suitable for the exchange of substances.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The capillary wall is only one cell thick (thin), shortening the diffusion distance; the capillaries branch into a huge network, providing a large surface area; the blood flow is slow, allowing more time for exchange.\nThese features allow oxygen, nutrients and wastes to be exchanged effectively between the blood and tissue fluid." },
      { q: "Explain why the wall of the left ventricle is thicker than that of the right ventricle.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The left ventricle has to pump blood to the whole body, which is a longer distance with greater resistance and needs more force; the right ventricle only pumps blood to the lungs, a shorter distance, so less force is needed; therefore the left ventricular wall is thicker." },
      { q: "Write down the function of the coronary arteries, and explain the possible consequences of their blockage.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The coronary arteries supply oxygen and nutrients to the heart muscle.\nIf the coronary arteries are blocked or narrowed by fat deposits (atheromas), the heart muscle is under-supplied with blood and oxygen; without oxygen the heart muscle may die (coronary heart disease / heart attack)." },
      { q: "Describe the process of blood clotting, and write down its importance to the body.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "When injured, platelets gather at the wound and release clotting factors; fibrinogen is converted into insoluble fibrin, which forms a network trapping blood cells and pathogens to form a clot.\nImportance: prevents further blood loss; forms a barrier preventing pathogens from entering; provides a basis for wound healing." },
      { q: "Write down the components of blood, and describe the main functions of plasma.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Blood consists of plasma and blood cells (red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets).\nPlasma is mainly water; it transports dissolved nutrients (glucose, amino acids), gases, urea, hormones and minerals to all parts of the body and carries away wastes." },
      { q: "Compare the blood in the pulmonary artery with that in the pulmonary vein.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood (low oxygen, high carbon dioxide) from the right ventricle to the lungs; the pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood (high oxygen, low carbon dioxide) from the lungs back to the left atrium.\n(In the circulation, the pulmonary artery is the only artery carrying deoxygenated blood, and the pulmonary vein is the only vein carrying oxygenated blood.)" },
      { q: "Explain why the heart rate and cardiac output increase during exercise.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "During exercise the muscles need more energy and carry out more respiration, producing more carbon dioxide;\nThe rise in blood carbon dioxide and the fall in pH stimulate the cardio-acceleratory centre, which through sympathetic nerves makes the heart muscle contract faster and more strongly;\nadrenaline also speeds up the heart. The heart rate and cardiac output increase, promoting oxygen transport to the muscles and faster removal of carbon dioxide." },
      { q: "Write down three functions of the lymphatic system.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "1. Returning excess tissue fluid to the blood.\n2. Transporting fats absorbed from the small intestine (via lacteals).\n3. Lymph nodes filter and destroy bacteria in the lymph (participating in immunity)." },
      { q: "Why must blood be matched before a transfusion? Explain the consequence of transfusing incompatible blood.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Blood of different groups contains different antigens and antibodies.\nIf incompatible blood is transfused, antibodies in the recipient's blood bind to the antigens on the donor's red blood cells, causing agglutination (clumping) of red blood cells, blocking blood vessels and destroying red blood cells, which may be fatal.\nTherefore blood must be matched to ensure compatibility." },
      { q: "Describe the sequence of atrial and ventricular contraction and relaxation in the cardiac cycle, and the role of the valves.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Cardiac cycle: atrial contraction (pumping blood into the ventricles) → ventricular contraction (pumping blood out of the heart) → atrial and ventricular relaxation (blood entering).\nThe atrioventricular valves prevent blood flowing back from the ventricles into the atria; the semilunar valves prevent blood flowing back from the arteries into the ventricles, ensuring one-way flow of blood." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 10,
    name: "Nutrition and Gas Exchange in Plants",
    questions: [
      { q: "Write down three features/adaptations of the root for absorbing water and minerals, and explain each.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "The epidermal cells have no cuticle, so water can easily enter the root.\nMany root branches and root hairs increase the surface area for absorbing water and minerals.\nRoot hairs are long and thin, growing between soil particles to absorb water and minerals from around them.\nMinerals can be absorbed against the concentration gradient by active transport, so absorption is possible even when the ion concentration in the soil is low." },
      { q: "Compare the gas exchange of a plant leaf in the daytime (in light) and at night (in darkness), and explain the significance of the compensation point.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "At night there is no light, so the plant stops photosynthesis and only carries out respiration: net intake of oxygen and net release of carbon dioxide.\nIn the daytime, when the light intensity is above the compensation point, the rate of photosynthesis is higher than the rate of respiration: the plant takes in carbon dioxide net and releases oxygen net, and makes food.\nThe compensation point is the moment when the rate of photosynthesis equals the rate of respiration, and the plant has no net gas exchange.\nOnly when photosynthesis (food production) exceeds respiration (food consumption) does the plant produce a net gain of food, which is essential for its survival and growth." },
      { q: "Write down the main structures inside a leaf and their functions.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Cuticle: contains wax, reducing water loss and protecting the leaf.\nPalisade mesophyll: upper layer, closely packed cells containing many chloroplasts, carrying out photosynthesis.\nSpongy mesophyll: lower layer, irregular loosely packed cells with air spaces, containing fewer chloroplasts, carrying out gas exchange and some photosynthesis.\nVascular bundles: xylem (water transport) and phloem (food transport).\nGuard cells: contain chlorophyll and control the opening and closing of stomata." },
      { q: "Explain the mechanism of stomatal opening and closing (the role of guard cells).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Guard cells contain chlorophyll and have unevenly thickened cell walls (the wall nearer the pore is thicker).\nIn the light, guard cells carry out photosynthesis, the sugar concentration inside rises, the water potential falls, and they take in water and swell; because the outer wall is thinner and swells more, the guard cells curve and the stomata open.\nAt night / when short of water, the guard cells lose water and become soft, and the stomata close.\nThe opening and closing of stomata regulate the entry of carbon dioxide and the loss of water." },
      { q: "Write down the factors affecting the absorption of water and minerals by plants, and explain the role of active transport in mineral absorption.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Factors: water potential of the soil, mineral concentration, surface area of root hairs, transpiration rate (affecting water transport), root respiration (providing energy).\nMinerals can be absorbed against the concentration gradient by active transport, so absorption is possible even when the soil ion concentration is low; active transport requires ATP produced by root respiration." },
      { q: "Compare the structure and function of palisade mesophyll and spongy mesophyll.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Palisade mesophyll: located in the upper layer, column-shaped cells closely packed, containing many chloroplasts; it is the main site of photosynthesis.\nSpongy mesophyll: located in the lower layer, irregular loosely packed cells with air spaces, containing fewer chloroplasts; it mainly carries out gas exchange and some photosynthesis." },
      { q: "Write down the gas exchange sites of plants (for different types of plants).", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Stomata on leaves or on the stems of herbaceous plants;\nlenticels on the stems of woody plants;\nthe epidermis of roots (root hairs provide a large surface area)." },
      { q: "Explain why plants need nitrogen, magnesium and iron, and the symptoms when these elements are deficient.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Nitrogen (nitrate): to make proteins, nucleic acids and chlorophyll; deficiency → yellowing of leaves, slow growth.\nMagnesium: a component of chlorophyll; deficiency → interveinal yellowing of leaves (insufficient chlorophyll).\nIron: needed to make chlorophyll; deficiency → yellowing of leaves.\n(Deficiency of both nitrogen and iron causes yellowing of leaves because chlorophyll synthesis is affected.)" },
      { q: "Describe experiments to show that photosynthesis requires light and carbon dioxide, including the design and results.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Requires light: destarch a plant by keeping it in the dark for a day, cover part of a leaf with opaque paper, expose to light for several hours; remove the leaf, decolourise and test with iodine. Result: the exposed part turns blue-black (contains starch), the covered part does not change. Conclusion: photosynthesis requires light.\nRequires carbon dioxide: place two shoots in two sealed jars, one containing sodium hydroxide (absorbs CO₂) and one containing water; expose to light and test the leaves with iodine. Result: the leaf with CO₂ turns blue-black, the leaf without CO₂ does not. Conclusion: photosynthesis requires carbon dioxide." },
      { q: "Explain why the upper epidermis of a leaf usually has fewer stomata than the lower epidermis.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The upper epidermis is directly exposed to sunlight; many stomata there would increase water loss (transpiration). Having fewer stomata on the upper epidermis reduces water loss, while stomata on the lower epidermis still allow carbon dioxide to enter for photosynthesis; this is an adaptation to reduce water loss." },
      { q: "Write down four features of a leaf that help it absorb light energy.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The leaf blade is broad and flat (increasing the area for light); the leaf is very thin (shortening the diffusion distance); the network of veins supports the leaf blade (spreading the leaf); palisade mesophyll is located in the upper layer and contains many chloroplasts (close to the light source, absorbing the most light)." },
      { q: "Explain why the rate of photosynthesis reaches an upper limit (saturation) in strong light and does not rise indefinitely.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "When the light intensity increases to a certain level, the rate of photosynthesis is limited by other factors (such as carbon dioxide concentration, temperature and enzyme quantity) and no longer rises; at this point carbon dioxide supply or enzyme catalysis becomes the limiting factor." },
      { q: "A student tested a leaf for starch with iodine solution.\n(a) Why must the plant be kept in the dark for a period of time before the experiment?\n(b) Why must the leaf be treated with boiling water and hot alcohol?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) To use up (destarch) the starch originally stored in the leaves, ensuring that any starch detected was produced by photosynthesis during the experiment.\n(b) Boiling water kills the cells and stops enzyme action; hot alcohol removes chlorophyll so that the colour change with iodine is clearly visible." },
      { q: "Explain why aquatic plants (such as Elodea) release bubbles in the light, and state the gas in these bubbles.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Aquatic plants carry out photosynthesis in the light, and the photolysis of water produces oxygen; the oxygen is released from the leaf surface as bubbles.\nThe bubbles are mainly oxygen. (The stronger the light and the higher the rate of photosynthesis, the more bubbles.)" },
      { q: "Write down the difference between autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition, illustrating with plants and animals.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Autotrophic nutrition: organisms (such as green plants) carry out photosynthesis, synthesising organic food from inorganic substances (carbon dioxide and water).\nHeterotrophic nutrition: organisms (such as animals and fungi) need to take in ready-made organic food to obtain nutrients.\nAutotrophs are the producers in food chains, providing food for heterotrophs." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 11,
    name: "Transpiration, Transport and Support in Plants",
    questions: [
      { q: "On a hot, sunny afternoon, a herbaceous plant (supported mainly by the turgor pressure of parenchyma cells) becomes wilted and the stem bends. Explain this phenomenon.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "On a hot, sunny day, the transpiration rate of the plant is higher than the rate of water absorption.\nThe parenchyma cells lose water and lose their turgidity (become soft and shrunken).\nThe turgor pressure of the parenchyma cells is the main source of support for herbaceous plants.\nTherefore the stem loses support and bends, and the plant wilts.\nIn contrast, woody plants are supported by the hardness of the xylem and are not affected by water content, so they remain upright." },
      { q: "Describe and explain two adaptations of xylem vessels as structures for transporting water.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Xylem vessels are hollow tubes, allowing water to flow with low resistance.\nXylem vessels have thickened / lignified walls that can withstand the negative pressure of the transpiration pull, preventing the xylem vessels from collapsing.\n(Alternatively: there are no cross-walls between cells, so water can flow freely upwards.)" },
      { q: "Describe the importance of transpiration, and write down the factors affecting the rate of transpiration.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Importance of transpiration: produces a cooling effect, preventing the plant from overheating; drives the upward transport of water and minerals in the xylem vessels; promotes the absorption of water and minerals by the roots.\nAffecting factors: light intensity (the stronger the light, the faster the transpiration), temperature (the higher, the faster), humidity (the higher, the slower), wind speed (the stronger the wind, the faster)." },
      { q: "Explain how plants reduce water loss (four adaptations).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The upper and lower epidermises have a cuticle (containing wax) that reduces evaporation;\nterrestrial plants have fewer stomata on the upper epidermis than on the lower epidermis;\nstomata are controlled by guard cells and close when water is short;\nsome plants only open their stomata at night (avoiding water loss during the hot day)." },
      { q: "Compare the xylem and the phloem in terms of structure and function.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Xylem: composed of xylem vessels (hollow, without cytoplasm, thick lignified walls), transporting water and minerals, mainly upwards (by the transpiration pull), and providing support.\nPhloem: composed of sieve tubes and companion cells (living cells), transporting organic nutrients such as sucrose from 'sources' to 'sinks' (translocation), in either direction." },
      { q: "Explain how the transpiration pull makes water move upwards in the xylem.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Transpiration from the stomata in the leaves loses water, lowering the water potential of the mesophyll cells;\nwater enters the mesophyll cells from the xylem vessels by osmosis, creating a negative pressure (transpiration pull) at the top of the vessels;\nbecause of the cohesion between water molecules, the water column is pulled continuously upwards, and water is drawn from the roots to replace it, forming a continuous flow from root to leaf." },
      { q: "Write down the difference in support between herbaceous plants and woody plants.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Herbaceous plants (non-woody parts) rely mainly on the turgidity (turgor pressure) of the parenchyma cells in the cortex and pith for support; they wilt when short of water.\nWoody plants rely mainly on the hardness of the lignified (thick-walled) xylem for support, which is not affected by water content, so they can remain upright." },
      { q: "A student placed a leafy shoot in water and added red ink; after a few hours he cut a cross-section of the stem and observed it.\n(a) Explain why the xylem was stained red.\n(b) Why was the phloem not stained red?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) The red ink entered the xylem vessels through the cut surface/roots and rose with the water, so the xylem was stained red, showing that water is transported through the xylem.\n(b) The phloem mainly transports organic nutrients (sucrose), not water; water (and the red ink) is transported through the xylem, so the phloem was not stained red." },
      { q: "Describe the principle of a potometer, and explain how to use it to compare transpiration rates in different environments.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "A potometer is a tube filled with water connected to a leafy shoot, containing an air bubble.\nTranspiration from the leaves pulls the water in the tube, moving the air bubble.\nRecord the time taken for the bubble to move a certain distance, from which the rate of water uptake (reflecting the transpiration rate) can be calculated.\nRepeat the measurement under different environments (different light, temperature, humidity, wind speed) for comparison." },
      { q: "Explain why a plant closes its stomata in dry weather, and the effect of this on photosynthesis.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In dry weather the plant loses too much water, so the guard cells lose water and the stomata close, reducing water loss (transpiration) and preventing the plant from wilting.\nClosing the stomata also reduces the entry of carbon dioxide into the leaf, so the supply of the raw material for photosynthesis decreases and the rate of photosynthesis falls. The plant has to balance water conservation with food production." },
      { q: "Write down the definition of translocation, and explain how sucrose is transported from a 'source' to a 'sink'.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Translocation is the process by which a plant transports organic nutrients (such as sucrose) in the phloem from a 'source' (where nutrients are made/released, e.g. green leaves) to a 'sink' (where they are used/stored, e.g. roots, fruits, seeds).\nSucrose is transported through the sieve tubes from the source to the sink; the source–sink relationship changes with the season and the growth stage." },
      { q: "Explain why woody plants can grow taller than herbaceous plants.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Woody plants have large amounts of lignified xylem (thick-walled tissue) that provides rigid mechanical support, able to support tall trunks;\nthe xylem also transports water to higher positions. Herbaceous plants are supported mainly by turgor pressure and cannot grow very tall." },
      { q: "A student used two potometers, one placed in front of a fan and one in still air. Predict and explain the difference in the rate of movement of the air bubbles.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The bubble in front of the fan moves faster.\nThe wind blows away the water vapour near the leaf surface, increasing the water-vapour concentration gradient between the leaf and the air, speeding up evaporation, so the transpiration rate increases and the rate of water uptake (bubble movement) also increases." },
      { q: "Explain why the transpiration rate of a plant is lower in humid weather.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "In humid conditions, the water-vapour concentration gradient between the leaf surface and the air is smaller, so evaporation is slower and the transpiration rate is lower.\n(Conversely, in dry weather the gradient is larger and transpiration is faster.)" },
      { q: "Write down the pathway of water from absorption by the roots to loss by transpiration in the leaves.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Soil water → root hair cells (by osmosis) → cortex cells → xylem vessels (transported upwards by the transpiration pull) → leaf veins → mesophyll cells → stomata/cuticle → lost to the atmosphere as water vapour." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 12,
    name: "Cell Cycle and Cell Division",
    questions: [
      { q: "Compare mitosis and meiosis in terms of process and outcome, and explain the importance of meiosis to sexual reproduction.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Process: mitosis involves one division; meiosis involves two successive divisions (I and II).\nMitosis has no pairing of homologous chromosomes; meiosis has pairing and crossing over of homologous chromosomes in prophase I.\nIn mitosis, sister chromatids separate in anaphase; in meiosis, homologous chromosomes separate in the first division and sister chromatids separate in the second.\nOutcome: mitosis produces two daughter cells identical to the mother cell (diploid); meiosis produces four haploid gametes with half the number of chromosomes.\nImportance: meiosis halves the chromosome number of gametes so that the original diploid number is restored after fertilisation; and it increases genetic variation through independent assortment, crossing over and random fertilisation." },
      { q: "Explain why the body cells of a person with Down syndrome have one extra chromosome 21.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "People with Down syndrome have three chromosomes in pair 21 (one extra).\nCause: when the gamete was formed by meiosis, the two members of pair 21 failed to separate.\nThe abnormal gamete carrying the extra chromosome 21 fused with a normal gamete.\nA zygote with three chromosome 21s was formed." },
      { q: "Describe the main events of interphase and the four stages of mitosis (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase).", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Interphase: cell growth, protein synthesis, formation of new organelles, DNA replication.\nProphase: chromosomes become shorter and thicker, the nuclear membrane breaks down.\nMetaphase: chromosomes line up at the equator, with centromeres attached to the spindle fibres.\nAnaphase: spindle fibres contract, sister chromatids separate and are pulled to opposite poles.\nTelophase: new nuclear membranes form, chromosomes loosen; cytoplasmic division." },
      { q: "Explain how meiosis produces genetic variation (independent assortment, crossing over and random fertilisation).", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Independent assortment: when gametes form, pairs of alleles on homologous chromosomes separate randomly, and genes on different chromosomes can combine freely, producing gametes with different gene combinations.\nCrossing over: in prophase I of meiosis, non-sister chromatids exchange segments, producing new gene combinations.\nRandom fertilisation: male and female gametes with different gene combinations fuse randomly, forming zygotes of different genotypes, increasing genetic variation." },
      { q: "Write down the number of chromosomes in human body cells and gametes, and explain why the chromosome number does not double generation after generation after fertilisation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Human body cells have 46 chromosomes (23 pairs); gametes (sperm and egg) have 23.\nMeiosis halves the chromosome number of gametes; when two haploid gametes fuse at fertilisation, the zygote restores 46 chromosomes (diploid), so the chromosome number is maintained from generation to generation." },
      { q: "Explain the importance of mitosis to multicellular organisms.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Mitosis produces two daughter cells genetically identical to the mother cell (diploid), used for:\n1. Growth (increasing the number of cells).\n2. Repair and regeneration of tissues (replacing damaged or dead cells).\n3. Asexual reproduction (producing genetically identical offspring)." },
      { q: "A student observed the tip of a root under a microscope and saw cells at different stages.\n(a) Why is the root tip suitable for observing cell division?\n(b) How can he identify cells undergoing mitosis?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) The meristematic cells in the root tip divide actively, so cells at many stages of division can be observed.\n(b) Chromosomes can be seen in the cells (deeply stained structures); different cells are at different stages (chromosomes aligned at the equator, separating and moving to the poles, etc.), so prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase can be distinguished." },
      { q: "Explain the difference between homologous chromosomes and sister chromatids.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Homologous chromosomes: a pair of chromosomes similar in size, shape, centromere position and gene arrangement, one from the father and one from the mother, which may carry different alleles.\nSister chromatids: the two chromatids formed by replication of a chromosome, genetically identical, joined at the centromere, separated in anaphase of mitosis." },
      { q: "Write down the relationship between cancer and cell division, and explain the characteristics of cancer cells.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Cancer is caused by uncontrolled cell division; mutations in the DNA related to cell division make cells lose growth control.\nCharacteristics of cancer cells: divide uncontrollably, forming tumours; malignant tumours can invade other tissues and metastasise." },
      { q: "Explain why meiosis only occurs in the reproductive organs (the sites of gamete production).", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The role of meiosis is to produce haploid gametes (sperm and egg) for fertilisation in sexual reproduction; only the germ cells in the reproductive organs carry out meiosis, while body cells carry out mitosis for growth and repair." },
      { q: "Describe the main stages of the cell cycle, and explain the importance of interphase to cell division.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The cell cycle consists of interphase and mitosis.\nInterphase: cell growth, synthesis of proteins and organelles, and DNA replication, preparing for division.\nIf DNA is not replicated in interphase, the daughter cells cannot receive the complete genetic material during division, so interphase is essential to cell division." },
      { q: "A woman's pair 21 chromosomes failed to separate, forming an egg with an extra chromosome 21. What chromosome number may her children with a normal man have? Explain.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The abnormal egg contains 24 chromosomes (one extra chromosome 21); the normal sperm contains 23.\nAfter fertilisation the zygote contains 47 chromosomes (one extra chromosome 21), i.e. Down syndrome.\nIf a normal egg (23) fuses with a normal sperm, the child has 46 chromosomes (normal)." },
      { q: "Write down the genetic difference between the offspring of asexual and sexual reproduction, giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Asexual reproduction: the offspring are genetically identical to the parent (no genetic recombination), e.g. reproduction by potato tubers, binary fission of bacteria.\nSexual reproduction: the offspring differ genetically from the parents (through meiosis and fertilisation), e.g. humans, flowering plants reproducing by seeds.\nThe offspring of sexual reproduction have greater genetic diversity and are better able to adapt to environmental changes." },
      { q: "Explain the relationship between chromosomes, DNA, genes and alleles.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Chromosomes are made of DNA and proteins and are the carriers of genetic material;\na gene is a segment of DNA on a chromosome and is the basic unit of inheritance;\ndifferent forms of a gene controlling the same trait at the same position on homologous chromosomes are called alleles;\nthe total DNA of a cell carries the genetic information of the organism." },
      { q: "Describe how to observe and calculate the mitotic index of cells in the laboratory, and explain why the average of several fields of view should be taken.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Prepare a temporary mount of the root tip (stained), and under the microscope randomly select several fields of view to count the proportion of cells undergoing division among the total cells (mitotic index).\nTaking the average of several fields of view reduces sampling error and makes the result more representative and reliable." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 13,
    name: "Reproduction in Flowering Plants",
    questions: [
      { q: "Compare the features of insect-pollinated and wind-pollinated flowers, and explain how these features help pollination.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Insect-pollinated flowers: large, brightly coloured petals with fragrance and nectar, attracting insects; pollen is sticky and heavy, adhering to insects' bodies, and the number of pollen grains is relatively small.\nWind-pollinated flowers: small or inconspicuous petals, no nectar; anthers hang out of the flower, pollen is light, dry and numerous, easily blown by the wind; stigmas are large and feathery, increasing the chance of catching pollen.\nThese features adapt the flowers to pollination by insects or wind respectively." },
      { q: "In mid-19th century Ireland, potato crops were severely infected by a pathogen, causing many deaths. Explain why vegetative reproduction of potatoes is regarded as the culprit for the crops being easily infected.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The offspring produced by vegetative reproduction have the same genetic make-up as the parent.\nThis provides limited raw material for natural selection of resistant strains.\nIf the parent's genotype is vulnerable to the pathogen, the whole population is identical and can be wiped out at once." },
      { q: "Describe the process by which pollen reaches the ovule and fertilisation is completed.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Pollen germinates on the stigma, forming a pollen tube.\nThe pollen tube grows down the style and reaches the ovule through the micropyle.\nThe male gametes in the pollen tube are released.\nA male gamete fuses with the female gamete (egg cell) in the ovule (fertilisation).\nThe zygote develops into an embryo." },
      { q: "Explain the developmental results of the flower parts (ovule, integument, ovary, ovary wall) after fertilisation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Ovule → seed; integument → seed coat; ovary → fruit; ovary wall → fruit wall (pericarp).\nThe zygote develops into an embryo.\nFunction of the fruit: protecting the seeds and helping in their dispersal." },
      { q: "Write down three ways in which seeds are dispersed, with one seed feature for each.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "1. Dispersal by animals: seeds have hooks (attaching to animals' fur) or the fruits are juicy (eaten by animals and the seeds excreted).\n2. Dispersal by wind: seeds are light and may have wings or hairs.\n3. Dispersal by explosive mechanism: ripe fruits burst and eject the seeds.\n(Or dispersal by water: seeds can float.)\nSeed dispersal avoids overcrowding and competition." },
      { q: "Compare asexual reproduction (vegetative propagation) with sexual reproduction, and explain why farmers often use vegetative propagation to grow certain crops.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Asexual reproduction: offspring develop from the vegetative parts of the parent, genetically identical to the parent, able to reproduce many identical offspring quickly, but lacking genetic variation.\nSexual reproduction: gametes are produced by meiosis and fertilisation forms a zygote; offspring differ genetically from the parents, with more variation.\nFarmers often use vegetative propagation (e.g. cuttings, tubers) because it preserves the parent's desirable traits, reproduces quickly and produces uniform offspring (e.g. seedless fruits, good varieties)." },
      { q: "Explain why cross-pollination increases genetic variation more than self-pollination.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Cross-pollination transfers pollen from one plant to the stigma of another plant, combining genes from different parents, so fertilisation produces offspring with greater genetic diversity; self-pollination only combines the genes of the same plant, so there is less variation." },
      { q: "Describe the conditions required for seed germination, and explain the role of each condition.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Water: makes the seed swell and the enzymes active, starting metabolism.\nOxygen: for respiration, releasing energy for germination and growth.\nA suitable temperature: allows the enzymes to work at their optimum.\n(Light is usually not essential; most seeds can germinate in the dark.)" },
      { q: "Explain why the dry mass of a seedling decreases at the beginning of germination, but starts to increase after green leaves appear.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "At the beginning of germination, the seedling has not yet grown green leaves and can only rely on the food stored in the seed.\nThe stored starch is broken down into sugars by enzymes and used up by respiration to provide energy, so the dry mass decreases.\nAfter green leaves appear, the seedling can carry out photosynthesis.\nWhen the rate of photosynthesis (food production) exceeds the rate of respiration (food consumption), there is a net gain of organic matter.\nTherefore the dry mass starts to increase." },
      { q: "Write down the parts of a flower and their functions (stamen, carpel, petals, sepals).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Stamen: the male reproductive organ; the anther produces pollen.\nCarpel (pistil): the female reproductive organ; the stigma receives pollen, the style connects the stigma and ovary, and the ovary contains ovules.\nPetals: attract pollinators (in insect-pollinated flowers).\nSepals: protect the flower bud.\n(Nectaries secrete nectar to attract insects.)" },
      { q: "Explain why flowers with many pollen grains are usually wind-pollinated.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Wind-pollinated flowers rely on wind to carry pollen to the stigma, where the success rate is low and most pollen is lost; producing large numbers of light, dry pollen increases the chance that pollen reaches a stigma, raising the chance of fertilisation." },
      { q: "A farmer found that his apple trees did not bear fruit. Explain the possible reasons and suggest solutions.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Possible reasons: there are no pollinators (insects) or no cross-pollinating variety, so fertilisation cannot occur;\nSolutions: introduce pollinators such as bees, plant mutually cross-pollinating varieties, or carry out artificial pollination, so that fertilisation occurs to form seeds and fruits." },
      { q: "Describe the process of double fertilisation, and explain its importance.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The pollen tube releases two male gametes: one fuses with the egg cell to form a zygote (developing into the embryo); the other fuses with the polar nuclei to form the endosperm (storing nutrients).\nDouble fertilisation is a feature of flowering plants, ensuring that the embryo receives nutrients (endosperm) for use during germination." },
      { q: "Write down the main parts of a seed and their functions.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Embryo: made up of the plumule (developing into the stem and leaves), radicle (developing into the root), hypocotyl and cotyledons; it is the young plant.\nSeed coat (developed from the integument): protects the embryo.\nEndosperm / cotyledons: store nutrients for use during germination." },
      { q: "Explain the significance of seed dormancy, and write down common ways to break dormancy.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Dormancy enables seeds to survive unfavourable conditions (such as drought and cold) and germinate only when conditions are suitable, increasing the chance of survival; it also allows seeds to germinate in batches at different times, reducing competition.\nWays to break dormancy: provide suitable water, temperature and oxygen, or treat with low temperature, scratch the seed coat, etc." }
    ]
  }
];
